# Providers and the Settings Dialog

Nullcast now reads from five content providers, and no install uses all of them. This note covers
the switch that decides which ones exist for a given install, and the shape of the dialog that
holds it.

## The provider switches

Each provider is **independently switchable**, stored in `services.json` under `providers`:

```json
{
  "providers": {
    "playlists": true,
    "nullcast_tv": false,
    "plex": true,
    "podcasts": true,
    "ytmusic": true
  }
}
```

| Key | Provider | Default |
|---|---|---|
| `nullcast_tv` | Nullcast.TV | **off** |
| `playlists` | the bookmarks service | on |
| `plex` | Plex Media Server | on |
| `podcasts` | Apple Podcasts | on |
| `ytmusic` | YouTube Music | on |

> [!NOTE]
> Everything that shipped before the switch existed defaults to **on**, so upgrading is a no-op —
> nobody's sidebar loses a tab. Nullcast.TV defaults to **off** because turning it on is a
> decision to sign in, not just a decision to look.

### What a switch controls

One flag, three consequences:

1. **The sidebar tab.** `ApplyProviderTabs` hides the tab outright. If the active tab is the one
   being switched off, the selection moves to the leftmost survivor.
2. **The Ctrl+K search source.** `RunSearchAsync` skips a disabled provider, its tab disappears
   from the palette, and the placeholder line names only what this install can actually search.
3. **Startup work.** A disabled provider is not touched at launch — Nullcast.TV does not even
   restore its session.

**History is not a provider.** It is an aggregator of whatever did play, it is always present,
and it is what guarantees the tab strip can never be empty.

### Enabled is not the same as configured

The two are deliberately separate states:

| | Plex | Nullcast.TV |
|---|---|---|
| Off | no tab at all | no tab at all |
| On, no credential | tab shows "No Plex server configured" | tab shows the sign-in prompt |
| On, configured | browses | browses |

A tab that is on but unconfigured is a **real state the UI explains**, not one it hides. Hiding it
would leave somebody who switched a provider on with no way to see why nothing happened.

## The settings dialog

### Why a left nav, not tabs

The old dialog was a top tab strip: General / Plex / Tether. Three fits; **ten does not** — the
strip wraps, and a wrapped tab row loses the one thing tabs are for, which is showing the whole
set at once. The rail is a grouped `ListBox` and grows down instead of across:

| Group | Pages |
|---|---|
| *(ungrouped)* | General |
| Providers | Nullcast.TV · Playlists · Plex · Apple Podcasts · YouTube Music |
| Advanced | Tether |

Each row uses the house list-item look — rounded card, accent tint, 3px left bar — so selection
reads the same here as it does in every list in the app. The right pane is a stack of
`StackPanel`s inside one `ScrollViewer`; `Nav_SelectionChanged` shows exactly one.

> [!NOTE]
> **Every provider gets a page whether or not it is switched on.** The page is *where you switch
> it on*, so hiding a disabled provider's page would make its own setting unreachable. Each page
> leads with its "Use …" checkbox and then explains itself.

### Sign-in moved out of the title bar

The main window used to carry **Connect to Playlist** / **Sign out** / the account name in its top
bar. With more than one account to hold, one pair of buttons could only ever speak for the first
of them, so each provider's account block now lives on its own settings page. The top bar keeps
the gear and nothing else.

### Two different commit semantics

The dialog does two kinds of thing, and they behave differently on purpose:

<dl>
  <dt><strong>Settings commit on Save</strong></dt>
  <dd>
    Provider switches, the Plex server and token, the Tether credentials, the personal access
    token, and the General page are gathered in <code>Save_Click</code> and written then.
    <strong>Cancel discards them.</strong>
  </dd>

  <dt><strong>Sign-in and sign-out apply immediately</strong></dt>
  <dd>
    They are actions against a remote service that have already happened by the time the button
    returns. There is nothing to "save" and nothing Cancel could undo. The dialog reports them
    through <code>PlaylistAuthChanged</code> / <code>NullcastTvAuthChanged</code>, and
    <code>OpenServices_Click</code> applies those <em>whichever way the dialog closed</em>.
  </dd>
</dl>

This is why **Test connection never commits**: a personal access token typed but not yet saved is
tested as-is through `NullcastTvService.TestCredentialAsync`, exactly as the Plex page tests a
typed server and token without persisting them.

## Adding another provider

1. Add the flag to `ProvidersConfig` (default `true` only if every existing install should keep
   working unchanged) and a read-only accessor on `ServicesStore`.
2. Add the member to `SidebarTab`, and to `IsTabEnabled` / `FirstEnabledTab` / `ApplyProviderTabs`.
3. Add the tab button and content pane to `MainWindow.xaml`, and the `SelectTab` wiring.
4. Add an `IntegrationSource` entry so History and the sidebar badge it in its own colour.
5. Add a settings page plus one `NavEntry` under "Providers".
6. If it is searchable: a `SearchSourceKind`, a tab button, a branch in `RunSearchAsync` /
   `RebuildResults`, and a case in `ActivateSearchResult`.

See [the Nullcast.TV notes](../nullcast-tv/integration-notes.hd) for a worked example of all six.
